<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berlin Conference</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Congo Conference</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> Conference was a meeting of European powers and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> convened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> from November 1884 to February 1885. It set rules for European claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, required effective occupation of coastal territory and recognised King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>. It accelerated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa">Scramble for Africa</a>, as powers rushed to sign treaties with African rulers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berlin-conference.jpg" alt="Berlin Conference" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adalbert von Roessler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kongokonferenz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Until the later 19th century European power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> was largely confined to the coast, where traders held posts protected by gunboats. In 1876 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-of-belgium">Leopold II of Belgium</a> founded the International African Association and invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> to join him; from 1878 to 1885 Stanley returned to the Congo as Leopold's agent.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> answered in 1881 by sending the naval officer Pierre de Brazza, who raised the French flag over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a>, and Portugal made a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> on 26 February 1884 to block the Congo Society's access to the Atlantic. Germany began expeditions of its own, which alarmed British and French statesmen. With British support and on Portugal's initiative, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> called representatives of thirteen European nations and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The conference met on 15 November 1884 and closed on 26 February 1885 with the signing of the General Act of Berlin. Fourteen countries sent plenipotentiaries, each with a single vote; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> reserved the right to accept or decline its conclusions. No African state took part.</p><p>Sultan Barghash bin Seyyid of Zanzibar asked to send a representative, but British pressure from the Anti-Slavery Society, which objected to Zanzibar's part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, led to refusal. Six of the fourteen countries, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, Sweden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, came home without any zone of interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><p>The General Act confirmed the holdings of Leopold's International Congo Society, and on 1 August 1885 the territory was named the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, a name absent from the Act. It decreed free trade among the signatories throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-basin">Congo Basin</a> and made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and Congo rivers free for shipping. Article 6 called on the powers to strive for the suppression of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><p>Under the principle of effective occupation a power could acquire rights only if it had treaties with local leaders, flew its flag and set up an administration, and it had to notify the other signatories of any possession or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> it took in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The Act concerned lands on the coast, and allowed a state to claim the hinterland after occupying the coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The conference is usually seen as formalising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa">Scramble for Africa</a>, and by 1914 about 90 per cent of the continent was under European control. Some historians, among them Sybil Crowe, Matthew Craven and Simon Katzenellenbogen, caution against overstating its role, pointing to the many bilateral agreements before and after it. The only borders set at the conference were those of the Congo region, and these were later revised.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Berlin Conference” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the <b>Berlin Conference</b> of 1884–1885 the association signed agreements with the powers recognising its control of most of the Congo basin, and the conference’s General Act of 26 February 1885 guaranteed free trade and navigation there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Berlin Conference</b> of 1884 assigned the territory to Germany, which allied with the king in 1897 and ruled through him and the existing hierarchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rwanda">Rwanda · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Berlin Conference</b> of 1884–1885 the colonial powers divided almost the whole continent among themselves, and by 1914 only Liberia and Ethiopia remained independent.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>France conquered Algeria from 1830, at the <b>Berlin Conference</b> of 1884–1885 European powers set rules for partitioning Africa, which they had almost wholly divided by 1914, and new empires joined the old: Japan took Taiwan in 1895 and Korea in 1910, and the United States took the Philippines…</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Berlin Conference</b> of 1884-1885 recognised French claims, and the territories were united with Gabon as the colony of Gabon-Congo, renamed French Congo in 1891.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-congo">French Congo</a></li><li><blockquote>British consul Edward Hewett signed protection treaties with rulers of the delta states in 1884, ahead of the <b>Berlin Conference</b>, and in June 1885 Britain proclaimed the Oil Rivers Protectorate.</blockquote><a href="/state/oil-rivers-protectorate">Oil Rivers Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
